    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Taylor Ward** stands as our premier play with a 71.19 score, anchored by a favorable matchup against the right-handed V\u00e1squez. While his raw base projection was moderate, a +16% park factor and a +19% boost from the opposing SP\u2019s high xERA make him an elite option. **Gleyber Torres** also anchors the top of our order with a 67.46 score; batting leadoff against Cantillo provides a massive efficiency boost, and his ability to leverage the platoon advantage makes him a cornerstone of today\u2019s production. Similarly, **Gavin Sheets** benefits significantly from the matchup against Gibson, yielding a 64.98 score driven by the high xERA of the opponent and a park environment that favors his power profile.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Matt Chapman**, whose 44.71 score fell just short of the threshold required to displace the current outfield/utility configuration. While **Matt Chapman** remains a high-floor asset, the specific combination of a tougher matchup against Brown and a lower batting order position dropped his efficiency relative to the volume of opportunities we expect from **Gavin Sheets** and **Jonathan Aranda**. Elsewhere, we are cautious about **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**; despite his status as a lineup staple, he faces a significant hurdle in Skubal, whose elite xERA of 2.82 suppressed **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**\u2019s score to 57.86. While his pedigree and BvP history allow him to stay in the lineup, we are tempering expectations for his raw output today.\n\nAs we monitor the board, we continue to prioritize volume and matchup-specific platoon advantages. **Geraldo Perdomo** provides surprising value at 64.73, largely due to the favorable matchup against Lowder. Conversely, we are keeping a close eye on **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Luis Arraez**, who both face the stingy Brown; their scores remain in the marginal-to-strong range, but the combination of high-skill pitching and suboptimal park factors suggests they are high-variance plays. We remain thin at the catcher position with **Cal Raleigh** still sidelined on the IL, placing additional pressure on our active bats to carry the load across the 5x5 categories.",
